            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Ruth Benedict</strong><br>\nRuth Benedict was born in 1887 in New York City. She started her academic journey in literature but was drawn to anthropology after attending a lecture by Franz Boas. She subsequently enrolled in graduate studies at Columbia University under Boas, which shaped her anthropological career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction to Anthropology</strong><br>\nUnder the mentorship of Franz Boas, Benedict developed her interest in the cultures of indigenous peoples. She was particularly fascinated by the ways that cultures shape individual behaviors and was among the first to apply the concepts of culture to the study of individual personality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Patterns of Culture\"</strong><br>\nBenedict's most famous work, \"Patterns of Culture,\" introduced the idea that each society has a \"personality\" reflected in the behaviors and attitudes of its members. Her comparative analysis of Native American cultures in this book was groundbreaking in anthropological theory.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to the \"Culture and Personality\" Movement</strong><br>\nBenedict was a key figure in the \"culture and personality\" movement in American anthropology. She believed that understanding individual personalities was crucial for comprehending the broader cultural patterns. Her research emphasized that cultural norms greatly influence personality development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and Anthropology</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Benedict applied her anthropological insights to the war effort. She wrote \"The Chrysanthemum and the Sword,\" which studied Japanese culture and society to aid American understanding of their wartime enemy, contributing to cross-cultural communication and military intelligence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Post-War Anthropology</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, Benedict's ideas influenced the burgeoning field of cultural anthropology. Her work encouraged other anthropologists to consider the importance of culture in interpreting social behaviors and personal relationships within different societies.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
