          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Teaching and Mentorship</strong><br>\nThroughout her career, Benedict was committed to teaching. She influenced a generation of anthropologists at Columbia University, imparting the importance of fieldwork and holistic cultural analysis, ensuring her methods and insights continued through her students.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Views on Cultural Relativism</strong><br>\nBenedict was a proponent of cultural relativism, arguing that one culture should not be judged by the standards of another. This view was instrumental in promoting a more respectful and empathetic approach to studying and interacting with different societies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Ruth Benedict</strong><br>\nRuth Benedict's contributions to anthropology persisted after her death in 1948. Her comparative approach to understanding cultural norms and the interplay between society and the individual remains influential, underlining the complexity and diversity of human cultures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
